 the close test, we can check that the file is closed after exhaustion.\n\nAlso test that stdin is not closed: after consuming from stdin, sys.stdin should still be open (StringIO has no close issue, but we can check `fh != sys.stdin` branch). Actually with StringIO, closing it wouldn't error, but we can assert it's not closed.\n\nLet me also consider binary mode: mode='rb' with iter_by bytes. In binary mode, readline returns b''... Actually with iter_by=LINEMODE and mode='rb', fh.readline returns bytes, and buf == '' comparison would be False for b'' \u2014 infinite loop? Wait: `if buf == ''` \u2014 in Python 3, b'' == '' is False, so it would never break... Actually that's a bug in the original code, but let's not test binary with LINEMODE. Let's test binary with iter_by=chunk size: fh.read(n) returns b'' at EOF, and b'' == '' is False... hmm, that would also infinite loop!\n\nWait, let me re-read: `if buf == '':  # EOF`. In Python 3, `b'' == ''` evaluates to False. So binary mode would infinite loop? Hmm, but the actual pytutils code... let me recall. The actual pytutils code:\n\n```python\ndef islurp(filename, mode='r', iter_by=LINEMODE, allow_stdin=True, expanduser=True, expandvars=True):\n    \"\"\"\n    Read [expanded] `filename` and yield each (line | chunk).\n\n    :param str filename: File path\n    :param str mode: Use this mode to open `filename`, ala `r` for text (default), `rb` for binary, etc.\n    :param int iter_by: Iterate by this many bytes at a time. Default is by line.\n    :param bool allow_stdin: If Truthy and filename is `-`, read from `sys.stdin`.\n    :param bool expanduser: If Truthy, expand `~` in `filename`\n    :param bool expandvars: If Truthy, expand env vars in `filename`\n    \"\"\"\n    if iter_by == 'LINEMODE':\n        iter_by = LINEMODE\n\n    fh = None\n    try:\n        if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n            fh = sys.stdin\n            fh_next = fh.readline\n        else:\n            if expanduser:\n                filename = os.path.expanduser(filename)\n            if expandvars:\n                filename = os.path.expandvars(filename)\n\n            fh = open(filename, mode)\n            fh_next = fh.readline if iter_by == LINEMODE else functools.partial(fh.read, iter_by)\n\n        while True:\n            buf = fh_next()\n            if buf == '':  # EOF\n                break\n            yield buf\n    finally:\n        if fh and fh != sys.stdin:\n            fh.close()\n```\n\nHmm, in the given code, line 29-30 area: `fh = sys.stdin` then `else:` \u2014 the given snippet doesn't show `fh_next = fh.readline` for stdin. Looking at the snippet:\n\n```\n        28:         if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n        29:             fh = sys.stdin\n                    else:\n        31:             if expanduser:\n```\n\nSo in the stdin path, fh_next is not set? Then line 40 `buf = fh_next()` would raise NameError... Hmm, wait. Actually in the real pytutils code:\n\n```python\n        if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n            fh = sys.stdin\n            fh_next = fh.readline\n```\n\nBut the snippet shows line 29 as `fh = sys.stdin` and line 31 as `if expanduser:`. So line 30 might be `fh_next = fh.readline` but it's not shown in the snippet (the snippet shows missing lines as gaps: 23, 24, then 26, 27, 28, 29, then 31...). Line 30 is missing from the snippet \u2014 it's probably `fh_next = fh.readline`. The snippet only shows lines that need coverage? No wait, the snippet shows the code with line numbers for lines 23-46, but line 30 is skipped in the display. Hmm, the display shows:\n\n```\n        23:     if iter_by == 'LINEMODE':\n        24:         iter_by = LINEMODE\n            \n        26:     fh = None\n        27:     try:\n        28:         if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n        29:             fh = sys.stdin\n                    else:\n        31:             if expanduser:\n        32:                 filename = os.path.expanduser(filename)\n        33:             if expandvars:\n        34:                 filename = os.path.expandvars(filename)\n            \n        36:             fh = open(filename, mode)\n        37:             fh_next = fh.readline if iter_by == LINEMODE else functools.partial(fh.read, iter_by)\n            \n        39:         while True:\n        40:             buf = fh_next()\n        41:             if buf == '':  # EOF\n        42:                 break\n        43:             yield buf\n                finally:\n        45:         if fh and fh != sys.stdin:\n        46:             fh.close()\n```\n\nLine 25 is blank, line 30 is `else:` (unnumbered), line 35 blank, line 38 blank, line 44 `finally:` (unnumbered). So line 30 is the `else:` line. Hmm, that means in the stdin path, fh_next is never assigned! Then `buf = fh_next()` at line 40 would raise NameError: name 'fh_next' is not defined.\n\nWait, but that can't be right for the real pytutils. Let me check the actual pytutils source:\n\nFrom pytutils (santinic/pampy helpers... actually pytutils is by ianw? Let me recall). pytutils/files.py:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport functools\n\nLINEMODE = -1\n\n\ndef islurp(filename, mode='r', iter_by=LINEMODE, allow_stdin=True, expanduser=True, expandvars=True):\n    \"\"\"\n    Read [expanded] `filename` and yield each (line | chunk).\n\n    :param str filename: File path\n    :param str mode: Use this mode to open `filename`, ala `r` for text (default), `rb` for binary, etc.\n    :param int iter_by: Iterate by this many bytes at a time. Default is by line.\n    :param bool allow_stdin: If Truthy and filename is `-`, read from `sys.stdin`.\n    :param bool expanduser: If Truthy, expand `~` in `filename`\n    :param bool expandvars: If Truthy, expand env vars in `filename`\n    \"\"\"\n    if iter_by == 'LINEMODE':\n        iter_by = LINEMODE\n\n    fh = None\n    try:\n        if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n            fh = sys.stdin\n            fh_next = fh.readline\n        else:\n            if expanduser:\n                filename = os.path.expanduser(filename)\n            if expandvars:\n                filename = os.path.expandvars(filename)\n\n            fh = open(filename, mode)\n            fh_next = fh.readline if iter_by == LINEMODE else functools.partial(fh.read, iter_by)\n\n        while True:\n            buf = fh_next()\n            if buf == '':  # EOF\n                break\n            yield buf\n    finally:\n        if fh and fh != sys.stdin:\n            fh.close()\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure about the stdin branch. Let me use get_info to check the actual code. The tool returns \"information about a symbol\" \u2014 let me call get_info('islurp') and get_info('LINEMODE').\n\nActually, the given snippet might be the actual code from the repo, and line 30 might indeed be missing `fh_next = fh.readline`. But wait \u2014 the snippet says \"lines 23-46 do not execute\" \u2014 meaning the whole function body doesn't execute because it's a generator and never consumed. The line numbers 23-46 refer to the file's line numbers. The unnumbered lines (`else:`, `finally:`) are shown without numbers probably because they're just formatting artifacts of the extraction.\n\nHmm, but if line 30 is `else:`, then the stdin branch lacks fh_next assignment. Let me check with get_info.\n\nActually, looking at the real pytutils on GitHub (jreese? no...). pytutils is \"pytutils - a small collection of python utilities\" by... I recall it's from https://github.com/mahmoudimus? Or it's used in \"howdoi\"? Actually pytutils is used by howdoi! howdoi depends on pytutils. Let me recall pytutils/files.py from howdoi's dependency:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport functools\n\nLINEMODE = -1\n\n\ndef islurp(filename, mode='r', iter_by=LINEMODE, allow_stdin=True, expanduser=True, expandvars=True):\n    \"\"\"\n    Read [expanded] `filename` and yield each (line | chunk).\n\n    :param str filename: File path\n    :param str mode: Use this mode to open `filename`, ala `r` for text (default), `rb` for binary, etc.\n    :param int iter_by: Iterate by this many bytes at a time. Default is by line.\n    :param bool allow_stdin: If Truthy and filename is `-`, read from `sys.stdin`.\n    :param bool expanduser: If Truthy, expand `~` in `filename`\n    :param bool expandvars: If Truthy, expand env vars in `filename`\n    \"\"\"\n    if iter_by == 'LINEMODE':\n        iter_by = LINEMODE\n\n    fh = None\n    try:\n        if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n            fh = sys.stdin\n            fh_next = fh.readline\n        else:\n            if expanduser:\n                filename = os.path.expanduser(filename)\n            if expandvars:\n                filename = os.path.expandvars(filename)\n\n            fh = open(filename, mode)\n            fh_next = fh.readline if iter_by == LINEMODE else functools.partial(fh.read, iter_by)\n\n        while True:\n            buf = fh_next()\n            if buf == '':  # EOF\n                break\n            yield buf\n    finally:\n        if fh and fh != sys.stdin:\n            fh.close()\n```\n\nI believe the real code has `fh_next = fh.readline` in the stdin branch. The snippet provided might have omitted it accidentally, or line 30 is that line but displayed without a number because... hmm, the display shows `else:` unnumbered between 29 and 31. If line 30 were `fh_next = fh.readline`, then `else:` would be on line 31 and `if expanduser:` on line 32. But the display shows 31 for `if expanduser:`. So in the given snippet, line 30 is `else:`.\n\nHmm wait, but the unnumbered lines: `else:` before 31, and `finally:` before 45. If `else:` is line 30 and `finally:` is line 44, then the numbering is consistent: 23, 24, (25 blank), 26, 27, 28, 29, 30=else, 31, 32, 33, 34, (35 blank), 36, 37, (38 blank), 39, 40, 41, 42, 43, 44=finally, 45, 46.\n\nSo in this version, the stdin branch does NOT set fh_next. That means consuming from stdin would raise NameError at line 40. Hmm, that seems like a bug, but maybe the actual pytutils code is different. Let me check with get_info tool \u2014 that's what it's for.\n\nActually wait. Maybe the real code is:\n\n```python\n        if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n            fh = sys.stdin\n            fh_next = fh.readline\n        else:\n```\n\nand the snippet extraction dropped the `fh_next = fh.readline` line? The task says \"The code below, extracted from pytutils/files.py\". The line numbers in the snippet (23-46) suggest the function starts at line ~22 in the file. Let me count the real file:\n\n```\n1: import os\n2: import sys\n3: import functools\n4: \n5: LINEMODE = -1\n6: \n7: \n8: def islurp(filename, mode='r', iter_by=LINEMODE, allow_stdin=True, expanduser=True, expandvars=True):\n...\n```\n\nHmm, the docstring is 9 lines. Let me count: def at line 8, docstring lines 9-17 (9 lines: \"\"\"Read..., blank, :param x5, \"\"\"), then `if iter_by == 'LINEMODE':` at line 18? That doesn't match 23.\n\nLet me count more carefully with the actual pytutils source. From GitHub (rgomesf? no). The actual pytutils repo: https://github.com/oliverhu? Hmm. I recall pytutils is by \"huanglianghua\"? Actually: https://pypi.org/project/pytutils/ \u2014 \"pytutils - Some handy utils\" by... I think it's from https://github.com/kevinjqiu? Not sure.\n\nLet me just count assuming the file header:\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport functools\n\nLINEMODE = -1\n\n\ndef islurp(filename, mode='r', iter_by=LINEMODE, allow_stdin=True, expanduser=True, expandvars=True):\n    \"\"\"\n    Read [expanded] `filename` and yield each (line | chunk).\n\n    :param str filename: File path\n    :param str mode: Use this mode to open `filename`, ala `r` for text (default), `rb` for binary, etc.\n    :param int iter_by: Iterate by this many bytes at a time. Default is by line.\n    :param bool allow_stdin: If Truthy and filename is `-`, read from `sys.stdin`.\n 