ctually wait, let me reconsider \u2014 maybe this is a known bug in pytutils. Let me check the real pytutils source on GitHub... From memory of pytutils (https://github.com/hujunxianligong? no...). The PyPI package pytutils by \"Cody Soyland\"? Hmm. Actually I recall pytutils is by \"Buck\" or from exocet author? Let me think \u2014 pytutils: \"Various python utilities\" by Kumar McMillan? Not sure.\n\nRegardless, get_info is authoritative here: the stdin branch doesn't set fh_next. So the correct test for lines 28-29 is one that expects NameError (documenting the current behavior), OR we skip... no, we need coverage.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, let me reconsider. Maybe I should test it as: consuming raises NameError, and importantly sys.stdin is not closed (finally branch). That covers lines 28, 29, 39, 40 (raises), 45 (False branch). Lines 39-40 are already covered by other tests, but the stdin-specific branches (28 True, 29, 45 False-via-stdin) get covered.\n\nBut is asserting NameError \"correct\"? The test verifies actual behavior. The task emphasizes tests must be correct and improve coverage. I think it's acceptable, and I should note it in a comment. Alternatively, use pytest.raises((NameError, ...))? No \u2014 be precise: NameError.\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider whether fh_next could be defined. In the else branch, fh_next is assigned. In the if branch (stdin), it's not. Python locals: fh_next is a local variable of the function (assigned somewhere). If the if-branch is taken, fh_next is unbound \u2192 referencing raises NameError (\"free variable\"? no \u2014 \"local variable 'fh_next' referenced before assignment\" \u2014 in Python 3.11+ it's \"cannot access local variable 'fh_next' where it is not associated with a value\", still NameError). Yes, NameError.\n\nSo the stdin test:\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_stdin_does_not_close_stdin(monkeypatch):\n    fake_stdin = io.StringIO(\"from stdin\\n\")\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, \"stdin\", fake_stdin)\n    gen = islurp(\"-\")\n    with pytest.raises(NameError):\n        next(gen)\n    assert not fake_stdin.closed\n```\n\nHmm, but this is testing buggy behavior. Should I instead make the test pass by providing a working path? There's no way to make the stdin path work given this code. Unless... we monkeypatch sys.stdin to something and also pre-define... no.\n\nWait, actually \u2014 hold on. Let me reconsider. What if `iter_by` is passed such that... no, fh_next is only assigned in the else branch. There's no way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I misread and the get_info output truncated something. The output shows the full function without \"...\" inside. The user's snippet also shows line 30 as `else:`. Both agree: no fh_next in stdin branch.\n\nOK so the stdin test asserts NameError. Actually, hmm, let me think about whether the task intends this. The task: \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches, always making sure that each test is correct and indeed improves coverage.\" Lines 28-29 are missing lines. To execute them, the generator must be started with filename='-' and allow_stdin truthy. The only observable outcome is NameError. So the test must expect that. I'll write it with a clear comment that this documents current (buggy) behavior: the stdin branch never assigns `fh_next`.\n\nHmm, wait, actually \u2014 one more idea. What if we pass a `mode` or something... no.\n\nWhat about monkeypatching `sys.stdin` to a real file object and... no, still NameError.\n\nOK, NameError test it is. Actually, let me double-check: could the exception be something else? `buf = fh_next()` \u2014 fh_next unbound local \u2192 NameError. Yes.\n\nBut hmm \u2014 actually, wait. Is `fh_next` maybe resolvable via the enclosing module globals if some other module... no. NameError.\n\nAlright. Now, also note LINEMODE = 0, not -1. So `iter_by == LINEMODE` \u2192 0. And `iter_by == 'LINEMODE'` string check. Fine.\n\nAlso note: with LINEMODE = 0, `functools.partial(fh.read, iter_by)` for iter_by=N>0. If someone passes iter_by=0... that equals LINEMODE \u2192 line mode. Not our concern.\n\nNow the binary mode infinite loop concern: with mode='rb', fh.readline() returns bytes; `buf == ''` \u2192 b'' == '' \u2192 False \u2192 infinite loop. So avoid binary mode tests. Also avoid iter_by with 'rb'. All my tests use text mode. Good.\n\nNow let me also double check `test_islurp_dash_no_stdin`: filename='-', allow_stdin=False \u2192 else branch \u2192 expanduser('-') = '-' (no ~), expandvars('-') = '-', open('-') \u2192 needs a file named '-' in cwd. monkeypatch.chdir(tmp_path) and create tmp_path/'-'. Creating a file named '-' is fine on Linux/macOS/Windows. Good. This covers the compound condition being False due to allow_stdin (second operand False). Note: branch coverage of `filename == '-' and allow_stdin`: outcomes True (stdin test), False (many tests). For condition coverage on the `and`, having allow_stdin=False with filename='-' exercises the short-circuit evaluation. Good to include.\n\nNow the tracking_open approach for asserting close. Alternative simpler approach: after consuming, assert we can... hmm, on all platforms, checking `fh.closed` requires a reference. tracking_open via monkeypatch on builtins.open is clean. But careful: patching builtins.open globally during the test could affect other machinery (e.g., importlib) \u2014 within a single test it's fine.\n\nActually, there's a subtlety: `monkeypatch.setattr(builtins, \"open\", tracking_open)` \u2014 the module under test calls `open(filename, mode)` which looks up builtins at call time \u2192 gets our wrapper. Good.\n\nAlternatively, I can avoid patching builtins by using `os`-level checks... no, tracking_open is fine and precise.\n\nLet me also assert in the missing-file test that no file handle leak... can't easily. Just assert raises OSError (IOError alias). FileNotFoundError is subclass of OSError. Use pytest.raises(OSError).\n\nNow, expanduser test: monkeypatch.setenv(\"HOME\", str(tmp_path)) and setenv(\"USERPROFILE\", str(tmp_path)). On POSIX, expanduser uses HOME. On Windows, USERPROFILE (or HOMEDRIVE/HOMEPATH, then HOME in newer versions). Setting both is safe. Note: monkeypatch.setenv(\"USERPROFILE\", ...) on POSIX is harmless.\n\nAlso expandvars test: setenv(\"PYTUTILS_TEST_DIR\", str(tmp_path)), filename \"$PYTUTILS_TEST_DIR/islurp_target.txt\". os.path.expandvars handles $VAR on all platforms. Use os.path.join? \"$VAR/name\" works on both since '/' works on Windows in Python. Fine.\n\nAlso test expanduser=False/expandvars=False explicitly with a path that WOULD be expanded if flags were True \u2014 to make the False branch meaningful. E.g., create file at tmp_path/'~literal' ... hmm, leading '~' only expands at path start. If filename = os.path.join(str(tmp_path), \"~u\", \"f.txt\")? expanduser only expands leading '~'. So a path starting with str(tmp_path) never expands. To make it meaningful: filename = \"~/f.txt\" with expanduser=False \u2192 open(\"~/f.txt\") relative to cwd \u2192 would fail unless a directory '~' exists. Too fragile.\n\nBetter meaningful approach for expandvars=False: create a file literally named \"$PYTUTILS_TEST_DIR_FILE\"? Hmm, '$' in filename is legal on Windows and POSIX. So: create tmp_path/\"$NOVAR.txt\", chdir to tmp_path, then islurp(\"$NOVAR.txt\", expandvars=False) opens the literal file. If expandvars were True, expandvars(\"$NOVAR.txt\") \u2192 \"NOVAR\" is unset \u2192 left unchanged \u2192 same result. Hmm, that doesn't distinguish either since the var is unset!\n\nTo distinguish: set the env var AND create a literal file with the same name. E.g., setenv(\"V\", \"X\"), create file \"$V.txt\" in cwd. With expandvars=True \u2192 opens \"X.txt\" (different file). With expandvars=False \u2192 opens literal \"$V.txt\". So the test: create both files, call with expandvars=False, assert content from literal file. That genuinely verifies expansion is skipped. Nice but maybe overkill; still, it's a stronger test. Let me do it \u2014 it's cheap.\n\nSimilarly for expanduser=False: create a directory literally named \"~\" in tmp_path? On Windows, creating a directory named \"~\" is allowed. chdir to tmp_path, create \"~\" dir containing \"inner.txt\", then islurp(\"~/inner.txt\", expanduser=False) \u2192 opens literal path \"~/inner.txt\" relative to cwd \u2192 works. With expanduser=True it would open tmp_path/inner.txt (different). Hmm, creating a \"~\" directory \u2014 os.makedirs(tmp_path / \"~\") works on Windows? Yes, '~' is a valid filename char on Windows. OK but this adds complexity. \n\nHmm, let me balance thoroughness vs. fragility. The core requirement: execute all missing lines and branches. Branch coverage needs expanduser=False and expandvars=False paths executed \u2014 a simple call with a plain existing file does that. The \"meaningful\" verification is a bonus. I'll do a middle ground: one test with expandvars=False reading a literal \"$VAR.txt\" file (with the env var set to something else), and one test with expanduser=False reading a literal \"~\" directory file. Actually, let me simplify: combine into one test with chdir.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 chdir + relative paths: islurp with expanduser=True on \"~/...\" \u2014 if I chdir to tmp_path and have \"~\" dir there, expanduser(\"~/inner.txt\") with HOME=tmp_path \u2192 tmp_path/inner.txt. If I also create tmp_path/inner.txt with different content, I can assert the literal one was read. That's a solid test. But is it too clever/fragile? Creating a \"~\" directory: `os.makedirs(os.path.join(str(tmp_path), \"~\"))` \u2014 fine everywhere. I'll include it but keep it simple.\n\nActually, you know, let me keep it simpler and more robust: \n\nTest A (expansion on): HOME/USERPROFILE \u2192 tmp_path; file at tmp_path/'exp.txt'; islurp('~/exp.txt') \u2192 content. And expandvars: setenv ISLURP_DIR; islurp('$ISLURP_DIR/exp.txt') \u2192 content. These verify lines 32/34 actually transform the path.\n\nTest B (expansion off): chdir(tmp_path); create literal files '~u.txt'... hmm '~' at start needed for expanduser. Create dir '~' with file 'off.txt' inside, and file '$V.txt'; setenv V=SHOULD_NOT_BE_USED. Then:\n- list(islurp('~/off.txt', expanduser=False, expandvars=False)) == ['literal tilde\\n']\n- list(islurp('$V.txt', expandvars=False)) == ['literal dollar\\n']\n\nWait, for '~/off.txt' with expanduser=False: expandvars=True would try expandvars('~/off.txt') \u2192 no $ \u2192 unchanged \u2192 open('~/off.txt') relative \u2192 the literal ~ dir. Fine. With both False \u2192 same. The point: if expanduser were True, it'd open tmp_path/off.txt which doesn't exist \u2192 error. So the test genuinely verifies expanduser=False. Good. And '$V.txt' with expandvars=False reads literal file; if expandvars were True \u2192 'SHOULD_NOT_BE_USED.txt' doesn't exist \u2192 error. Genuinely verifies. \n\nBut hmm \u2014 on Windows, is a directory named '~' creatable? Yes. Is '$V.txt' creatable? Yes. OK.\n\nActually, maybe I'm overengineering. Let me include Test B but keep it tidy. Actually, I realize there might be an issue with chdir and tmp_path cleanup on Windows (open handles) \u2014 we don't hold handles after tests (files closed by islurp; the literal files we create via write_text are closed). monkeypatch.chdir restores cwd after test. tmp_path cleanup happens later; no open handles. Fine.\n\nNow, the stdin NameError test. Let me write:\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_reads_stdin_placeholder(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"\n    filename='-' with allow_stdin=True selects sys.stdin (lines 28-29).\n    NOTE: the current implementation never assigns `fh_next` on this path,\n    so consuming the generator raises NameError. This test documents that\n    behavior while still executing the stdin selection lines and the\n    `fh != sys.stdin` guard in the finally block.\n    \"\"\"\n    fake_stdin = io.StringIO(\"stdin line 1\\nstdin line 2\\n\")\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, \"stdin\", fake_stdin)\n\n    gen = islurp(\"-\")\n    with pytest.raises(NameError):\n        next(gen)\n\n    # finally-block guard: stdin must NOT be closed\n    assert not fake_stdin.closed\n```\n\nHmm wait, but is this \"correct\"? It matches actual behavior. The task says tests must be correct and improve coverage. This executes lines 28-29 (previously uncovered) and the `fh != sys.stdin` False branch. I think it's the right call. But let me reconsider once more whether the real pytutils has `fh_next = fh.readline` there... The get_info output is the ground truth for this exercise. It shows no assignment. Also the user's snippet agrees. So NameError it is.\n\nHmm, actually hold on. Let me reconsider. What if get_info's \"...\" omitted a line? The output literally says '\"...\" below indicates omitted code.' \u2014 that's a generic header explaining notation; the shown code contains no \"...\". The function shown is complete and matches the user's snippet line-for-line (including line 30 = else). So no fh_next in stdin branch. Confirmed.\n\nOne more consideration for the stdin test: after NameError, the finally runs: `if fh and fh != sys.stdin:` \u2014 fh is fake_stdin, sys.stdin is fake_stdin \u2192 equal \u2192 False \u2192 no close. Assert not closed. Also assert the exception is NameError specifically.\n\nAlso \u2014 should the stdin test also verify that with allow_stdin=True and filename='-', sys.stdin was selected? The NameError indirectly proves the stdin branch was taken (else branch would have opened a file named '-' or raised FileNotFoundError). To make it airtight, ensure no file named '-' exists in cwd... cwd during tests is the repo root typically; a file named '-' is unlikely. But to be safe, monkeypatch.chdir(tmp_path) in that test too, so if the else branch were taken, open('-') would raise FileNotFoundError, not NameError. Then asserting NameError proves the stdin branch executed. \n\nNow the close-verification test with tracking_open. Let me write it:\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_closes_file_after_exhaustion(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    p = tmp_path / \"close_check.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"data\\n\")\n\n    real_open = builtins.open\n    opened = []\n\n    def tracking_open(file, mode=\"r\", *args, **kwargs):\n        fh = real_open(file, mode, *args, **kwargs)\n        opened.append(fh)\n        return fh\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(builtins, \"open\", tracking_open)\n\n    chunks = list(islurp(str(p)))\n\n    assert chunks == [\"data\\n\"]\n    assert len(opened) == 1\n    assert opened[0].closed  # finally: fh.close() executed\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `p.write_text` happens before patching. Good. But note: `tracking_open(file, mode=\"r\", ...)` \u2014 islurp calls open(filename, mode) positionally. Fine.\n\nHmm, one concern: monkeypatch.setattr(builtins, \"open\", ...) \u2014 does the module under test resolve `open` from builtins dynamically? Yes, global name lookup falls back to builtins at call time. Good.\n\nAlso, `opened[0].closed` \u2014 after list() exhausts the generator, the finally block ran \u2192 close() called \u2192 closed True. \n\nNow the bytes test:\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_iter_by_bytes(tmp_path):\n    p = tmp_path / \"chunks.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"abcdefghij\")\n    chunks = list(islurp(str(p), iter_by=4))\n    assert chunks == [\"abcd\", \"efgh\", \"ij\"]\n```\n\nCovers line 37 else (functools.partial). Also EOF break with '' from read. Good.\n\nString 'LINEMODE' test:\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_accepts_linemode_string(tmp_path):\n    p = tmp_path / \"lines.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"a\\nb\\nc\\n\")\n    assert list(islurp(str(p), iter_by=\"LINEMODE\")) == [\"a\\n\", \"b\\n\", \"c\\n\"]\n```\n\nCovers lines 23-24.\n\nDefault line test (covers most lines):\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_line_mode_default(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    p = tmp_path / \"sample.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"first\\nsecond\\nthird\\n\")  # no trailing newline on last? \"third\\n\" has trailing newline \u2192 readline yields \"third\\n\" then \"\" \u2192 break.\n    ...\n```\n\nLet me include a file without trailing newline too: \"first\\nsecond\\nlast\" \u2192 yields \"first\\n\", \"second\\n\", \"last\", then \"\" \u2192 break. Good, covers both.\n\nAlso laziness test? Not needed.\n\nMissing file test:\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_missing_file_raises_and_skips_close(tmp_path):\n    missing = tmp_path / \"nope.txt\"\n    with pytest.raises(OSError):\n        list(islurp(str(missing)))\n```\n\nCovers exception path + `fh` falsy short-circuit in finally (fh is None). Note: FileNotFoundError \u2282 OSError. Good.\n\nNow imports: `from pytutils.files import islurp, LINEMODE`. Do I need LINEMODE? I can use it in the string test comparison or pass iter_by=LINEMODE explicitly. Let me use it somewhere to justify the import \u2014 e.g., assert islurp default behavior equals iter_by=LINEMODE. Or just import it and use in one test: `list(islurp(str(p), iter_by=LINEMODE))`. Fine.\n\nAlso, should tests handle the case where pytutils isn't importable? Assume it is (the task context).\n\nLet me also think: does `islurp` exist at `pytutils.files.islurp`? Yes per the task.\n\nNow write the full test script:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for pytutils.files.islurp targeting lines 23-46 (full branch coverage).\"\"\"\n\nimport builtins\nimport io\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom pytutils.files import LINEMODE, islurp\n\n\ndef test_islurp_line_mode_default(tmp_path):\n    \"\"\"Default call: iterate a file line by line; covers open/readline/yield/EOF/close.\"\"\"\n    p = tmp_path / \"sample.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"first\\nsecond\\nlast\")  # no trailing newline on the last line\n\n    chunks = list(islurp(str(p)))\n\n    assert chunks == [\"first\\n\", \"second\\n\", \"last\"]\n\n\ndef test_islurp_explicit_linemode_and_string_linemode(tmp_path):\n    \"\"\"iter_by=LINEMODE and the literal string 'LINEMODE' behave identically.\"\"\"\n    p = tmp_path / \"lines